Date:     Mon, 6 Dec 93 23:12:37 GMT
From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject:  Zoning in VR 

******************************************************************

Pierre Uszynski brought up an interesting point about VR commercial
zoning. Spatial limitations wouldn't exist in an absolute sense,
but people have limited abilities to process information, which
means that "stores" might pay for being closely linked to
each other in information space. The same stores might be
in a number of different hypertextual and VR configurations.

I would think that a VR storefront would take up less "space"
along a "street" than a real one would, if only because I
think that people would have more endurance for just looking
at products than they do for walking and looking. (Or maybe
not--maybe walking helps keep you interested by moving more
oxygen to your brain and supplying kinesthetic variety.)

VR commercial districts wouldn't be limited to three spatial
dimensions--I can imagine indicating that I want to turn
andward or eckward* and seeing a new set of stores--the
limitation would be how complex a space people could orient
in, even with a cybernetic trail of crumbs.

Nancy Lebovitz

