Sigh. "Quirky macintosh formats". "More than ten percent of computers". While it  
is true that the Macintosh platform has been as blessed as the Dos/Win platform  
with a plethora of native file formats, Macs have had the advantage of having a  
few, well-defined generalized formats from the very beginning. PICT, PNTG, EPS.  
Almost *every* graphics-related piece of software ever written for the Mac  
understands at least one of these formats. And it has been possible, for at least  
the last 4-5 years, to get Postscript output from *any* Mac program that supports  
printing - a Postscript printer is not required. This is a well-documented and  
widely known feature, not some eclectic or deviously hidden easter egg.

Dependable, *free* file readers for GIF, JPEG, MPEG, and many other native  
formats (*both* PC and Mac) are available for the Mac platform. The same can not  
be said of their competition.

>I will begin collecting a list of freely available software
>for displaying and listening to files in various formats.
>If any of you out there have information relevant to this
>task, please mail me and I'll summarize to the list.
>Of special interest are FTP sites where such software
>can be found.

For the Next, I have had very good results with these programs.

MPEGPlay    (MPEG)
ImageViewer (atkras, gif, jpg, itex, pbm, pgm, ppm, macp, mtek, eps, ps,
             ras, rle, icon, tiff, face, xbm, lisp, brush, and ais)
ConvertPICT (Mac pict -> eps)

All of these are free or low-cost shareware and all are available on any of the  
major Next archive sites (cs.orst.edu, sonata.cc.purdue.edu, srawgw.sra.co.jp).  
There are literally dozens more, but I find that these cover just about all the  
bases I need (although I might go look for a dxf translator soon). If you feel  
like paying, then PixelMagician is by far the best bang for your buck. it can  
translate everything I've seen except for MPEG, and do image manipulation to  
boot.

For the Mac (all free or cheap (ie < $30)):
Imagery   (tiff,iff,grasp (gl),rle,pcx,bmp,pict,dxf,iges,cgm,gif,...*many* more)
QuickGIF  (GIF, PICT, PNTG and various sound formats)
GIFfer    (GIF, SCAN, QDV, PICT, and Mac resource)
ViewRLE   (rle)
Anim-8    (PICS (this is a mac-based movie format - basically a bunch of PICTs))
MoviePlayer (Quicktime)

Of these, Imagery is the clear champion. Jeff Lewis has spent a great deal of  
time and energy building a product that can read graphics files from Atari ST,  
Amiga, DOS, Windows, Unix (Sun, Xenix, XWindows), Apple II, Mac and several  
additional generic formats. I've listed only a few - maybe 20% of all that the  
program supports. About the only things it can't read are Quicktime and  
Postscript. And all he asks for it is a contribution to the user's local  
ASPCA/RSPCA - a request I'm sure the good businesspeople of this list would  
consider extremely reasonable, regardless of their feelings regarding charities.
Although it will let you view files, Imagery is primarily a conversion program.  
It lets the user save the file in any of several different formats, or, for real  
image studs, it'll even let you fool around with the raw data.

If you want commercial Mac image software, the list is too long and well-known to  
go into here. 


>If there are quicktime (doubtful) or mac-format image file
>viewers for pclones and unix boxes, that would be useful
>too.

I don't work with DOS-boxes anymore if I can help it, but I do know that there is  
_Quicktime for Windows_ and free/shareware Quicktime viewers available for  
DOS/Windows. And NeXTtime (Quicktime for NextStep) is set for release this  
winter.

Personally, I don't think setting standards in the sense of "These are the only  
acceptable formats" is necessary or wise. I think that the responsibilty of  
finding a program to view an image is upon the user who wishes to view it, as is  
the responsibility of obtaining a computer to view it on and making sure  
electricity and a suitable network connection is available. I have no problem  
with guidelines however, that would be of the "These are the easiest and most  
widely-supported formats in use. If you wish people to look at your images, you  
would do well to use one of these formats. If disk-space gets tight, files in the  
least-widely used formats are likely to be first on the chopping block."

I should note that with storage prices continuing to fall, I doubt that people  
will be worrying about still-image storage space for very long (sound and movies,  
on the other hand, are likely to be vexing for the next decade).

