Date: Mon, 6 Dec 93 17:01:02 PST
From: pierre@shell.portal.com (Pierre Uszynski)
Subject: Zoning in VR (a branch of VirtBiz)

There are several ways to see the problem. They are not just
interesting in a practical sense (after all a zoning commission
can just kick you out of their jurisdiction, whatever physical
form this jurisdiction has, as Tim May mentioned). But VR zoning
creates plenty of new ways that people can be obnoxious to each
other :-) or imaginative :-)

1) Topography is not limited. As Tim points out, addresses and pointers
can send you from system to system, whatever the "look" they try to
take. One could be a text mud, one email-based, and one Snow-Crash
World (Metaverse).

2) But the problem that Nancy Lebovitz (and Snow Crash) raise it
that there are limits to the geometries most customers would be
able to grasp. It's the usual UI and info system rule, 7 different
items will keep you busy enough (or whatever form the rule has in
your particular field). So, an Internet-like webs of thousands of
pointers does not look like much of a master plan for what will
become your favorite hangout. MUD designers have kept this in mind
so far, if only to ease the design, there are only so many
direction you want to go from anywhere, and still know your way
around. (We want to know our way around for browsing, and games of
all kinds).

3) Building new links, representations and interfaces is likely
to be easy where not restricted by the "landlord". Anybody can set up
a new store at any street corner of a current text-based mud: just
place a mud character there, with a sign, tune your description, and
wait for customers. In most "professional" VR settings I would expect
the store owners to be able to "remodel" by defining what the inside
of their store looks like.

Problem:

Because of (2), I would expect attractive game/browsing VR setups to
provide some kind of global architecture that solves the navigation
problem for their customers. Snow Crash's Metaverse makes that world
look like a planet, cities, streets, real-estate... There are a limited
numbers of stores, streets, etc.. at any single location (although the
wizards should feel free to bypass all this crap as Tim pointed out).

So, now the Metaverse has "more valuable" real-estate. That's for example
the places near major attractions, or on the main strip. That's where
zoning mattered for the city architects, they tried to help their
customers by solving navigation needs, meanwhile creating sites that
are more desirable than others (no they didn't mind :-). Because of the
browsing factor, stuff that takes (ahem) "space" (on your screen,
in your goggles, wherever) is more efficient (thus valuable) than,
say, hyperpointer buttons on some directory.

So, now, as virtual developper, I could buy in Metaverse a store front
on the strip, and remodel it to look like a street (could be a long
street with lots of other stores to rent or sell out). Or even a whole
new city area. After all physical size has not much meaning... who cares
if a store in toontoown contains a whole new city... And all that nice
planning by the founding fathers is down the drain. What looked like
a street nows is a whole new city. A street and a store remodeled into
a street may even look the same, no way to tell (for a basic user).


Worse (or more fun), as a more determined virtual developper,
I could decide that inside my store, the topography will change.
A visitor could enter, talk to somebody a minute or two,
look around and not recognize the place (in particular NO exit
back to the strip :-) Or, as a the
same determined developper I could post 200 characters with signs
all around the entrance to the white house. The characters would be
handing out VR catalogs and taking orders, not demonstrators for a
change (instant market on prime real-estate).


